    {
      "id": "0ee41eb7130e",
      "kind": "character",
      "chamber": 1,
      "seed": 1207283403,
      "data": {
        "lines": [
          "Mind your feet. The floor is only water pretending very hard to be floor.",
          "Everything down here glows for one of three reasons: come closer, go away, or simply I'm here. It is mostly the third.",
          "Lumen keeps asking how they light themselves without a wick. I tell him: loneliness, and a little chemistry.",
          "The moth collector came down once with a jar. Went back up with it empty. Said it wasn't fair to the dark.",
          "If you see a light that answers yours, don't blink. They don't get second chances and neither do you."
        ],
        "look": {
          "accent": "#ff5fd2",
          "coat": "#0b2b3a",
          "hat": "net"
        },
        "name": "Auntie Coral"
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014245679
    },
    {
      "id": "388107bf1182",
      "kind": "note",
      "chamber": 1,
      "seed": 3515247908,
      "data": {
        "text": "You asked what I wanted the deepest room to be. Not a room. A dark so complete that the only way to be found is to make yourself a lamp. Put something in it that has been shining for a thousand years with no reply, and make it still beautiful. Then let a visitor walk in with a lantern and watch what rises to meet them. — M.H., formerly of the lifeguard tower",
        "title": "Letters to the Architect, page 3"
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014245679
    },
    {
      "id": "c2c9eb72a3f9",
      "kind": "lanterns",
      "chamber": 1,
      "seed": 2897226829,
      "data": {
        "count": 5,
        "reward": "Somewhere beneath the floor, a slow green pulse answers: once, exactly once, for you."
      },
      "by": "command:claude",
      "at": 1792014245679
    }
